# Line endings, pinned.
#
# These trees reach the cell PC by hand copy from a Windows workstation, and a
# copy through an SFTP client in automatic/text mode rewrites whatever is on
# that client's text-extension list. The first cell bootstrap arrived with 224
# .py files rewritten to CRLF across eleven repositories, every one of which
# then read as an uncommitted change -- and the only way past the warning threw
# them all away, real edits included.
#
# `eol=lf` is the half that matters: it makes git write LF into the WORKING
# TREE no matter what core.autocrlf or core.eol say locally, so a checkout here
# cannot acquire CRLF from somebody's global git config.
#
# For the shell scripts this is not tidiness. bash does not tolerate CRLF at
# all: `set -euo pipefail\r` is an invalid option and the script dies before
# its first line runs, with an error that names neither the file nor the cause.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.bash    text eol=lf
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.repos   text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf

# Binary, so git never tries to normalise them.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.ply     binary
*.stl     binary
*.dae     binary
*.zip     binary
*.whl     binary
